<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kuraka">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuraka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Curaca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kuraka, or curaca, was an Andean aristocrat and magistrate who headed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllu</a> or a federation of ayllus, an office often but not always inherited; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> his authority was granted by the Inca. Kurakas collected taxes, allocated farmland, settled disputes, judged minor crimes and chose wives for unmarried men of 25 or over, and were exempt from taxation, with the right to several wives and to ride in a litter. Under Spanish rule they mobilised tribute and labour, and a kuraka, Tupac Amaru II, led the Andean rebellion of 1780–1781.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Below them the population was counted and organised in decimal units, from groups of 10 households up to 10,000, led by local lords, the <b>kurakas</b>, who were usually confirmed in office if loyal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Indigenous communities paid tribute and were governed through their hereditary lords, the <b>kurakas</b>, who mediated between villagers and Spanish officials, while the Andean population fell steeply in the sixteenth century through epidemics, war and forced labour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>curacas</b>, hereditary lords of the conquered peoples, governed their own people in the Inca’s name.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The crown recognised the old ruling families of central Mexico and the Andes as nobles so long as they were loyal and Catholic: caciques in New Spain, <b>kurakas</b> in Peru, where Inca nobles who proved their descent could be recognised as hidalgos of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
